<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apartheid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apartheid (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> for &quot;separateness&quot;) was the system of institutionalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a> and white minority rule imposed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> government from 1948 until the early 1990s. Laws classified the population by race, reserved land, jobs and political rights for whites, and assigned Africans to ethnic homelands. Internal resistance and international <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> led to negotiations and the first democratic election in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apartheid.jpg" alt="Apartheid" width="320" height="545" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guinnog · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DurbanSign1989.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> word apartheid means separateness, and its first recorded use was in 1929. The policy grew out of the racism of colonial factions and out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s industrialisation, in which segregation was developed to nurture early industry such as mining and cheap labour was taken from groups the state classed as peasants and migrants. By 1948 black political organisations and leaders, among them Alfred Xuma, James Mpanza, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> and the Council of Non-European Trade Unions, were demanding political rights, land reform and the right to unionise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Apartheid is commonly divided into petty apartheid, the segregation of public facilities and social life, and grand apartheid, which separated housing and employment by race. The first apartheid law was the Prohibition of Mixed Marriages Act of 1949, followed by the Immorality Amendment Act of 1950. The Population Registration Act of 1950 classified all South Africans as Black, White, Coloured or Indian according to appearance, known ancestry, socioeconomic status and cultural lifestyle. The Reservation of Separate Amenities Act of 1953 enforced segregation of public premises and services, and the Bantu Education Act of 1953 created a separate school system designed to prepare black people for lives as a labouring class.</p><p>Where people lived followed their classification. Between 1960 and 1983, 3.5 million Black Africans were removed from their homes and forced into segregated areas, most of them to restrict the black population to ten designated homelands, or bantustans. Four of these, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transkei">Transkei</a>, Bophutatswana, Venda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciskei">Ciskei</a>, were declared independent, but they had little economic infrastructure and were in practice controlled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. In the 1950s about 60,000 people were moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> to the new township of Soweto.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Apartheid met domestic and international opposition. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre">Sharpeville massacre</a> of 1960 brought more condemnation abroad, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and others imposed arms embargoes and economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>. Resistance inside the country grew more militant in the 1970s and 1980s, and from 1985 to 1989 serious political violence centred on the Black townships under states of emergency. Reforms under P. W. Botha, including a tricameral parliament, failed to satisfy most activists.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/f-w-de-klerk">F. W. de Klerk</a> announced on 2 February 1990 that he would repeal discriminatory laws and lift the 30-year ban on the leading anti-apartheid groups. The apartheid laws were abolished in 1991, and the first election with universal suffrage was held on 27 April 1994, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> winning 62.65 per cent of the vote. The legacy of apartheid persists in social and economic inequality, and the 1976 International Convention on the Suppression and Punishment of the Crime of Apartheid and the 2002 Rome Statute made apartheid a crime in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apartheid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Apartheid</b> South Africa became the target of one of the largest international campaigns of the twentieth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Apartheid</b> and its “Coloured” designation ended in 1994.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Abroad, a consumer boycott launched in London in 1959 became the Anti-<b>Apartheid</b> Movement after Sharpeville.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>During the 1970s and 1980s it was sung regularly at the meetings of the worldwide Anti-<b>Apartheid</b> Movement, which made it known far beyond Africa.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Apartheid</b> laws were extended after 1948, and the Odendaal Plan of 1964 set up ethnic homelands.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-west-africa">South West Africa</a></li></ul></section>
